NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:03.710
For this final video for this week, let's talk a bit about why

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:08.430
convolutions are so useful when you include them in your neural networks.

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:15.190
And then finally, let's briefly talk about how to put this all together and how you could train a

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:19.270
convolutional neural network when you have a labeled training set.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:26.590
I think there are two main advantages of convolutional layers over

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:28.950
just using fully connected layers.

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:33.670
And the advantages are parameter sharing and spasticity of connections.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:35.550
Let me illustrate an example.

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:42.390
Let's say you have a 32 by 32 by 3 dimensional image.

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:48.990
And this actually comes from the example from the previous video.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:53.470
But let's say you use a 5 by 5 filter with six filters.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:03.570
And so this gives you a 28 by 28 by 6 dimensional output.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:16.970
So 32 by 32 by 3 is 3072 and 20 by 20 by 6 if you multiply out those numbers is 4704.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:24.250
And so if you were to create a neural network with 3072 units in one layer and

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.730
you know 4704 units in the next layer.

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:32.970
And if you were to connect every one of these neurons, then the weight matrix, the number of

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:41.890
parameters in the weight matrix would be 3072 times 4000, which is about 14 million.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.450
So that's just a lot of parameters to train.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:49.170
And today you can train your networks with even more parameters than 14 million.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:54.170
But considering that this is just a pretty small image, this is a lot of parameters to

00:01:54.250 --> 00:02:01.170
train. And of course, if this were to be a 1000 by 1000 image, then this weight

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.930
matrix will just become infeasibly large.

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:11.610
But if you look at the number of parameters in this convolutional layer, each filter is

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:16.690
5 by 5. So each filter has 25 parameters plus a bias parameter.

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:20.690
That means you have 26 parameters per filter and you have six filters.

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:26.250
So the total number of parameters is that which is equal to 156 parameters.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:30.610
And so the number of parameters in this conv layer remains quite small.

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:37.650
And the reason that a confident has relatively small parameters is really two reasons.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:39.450
One is parameter sharing.

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:46.090
And parameter sharing is motivated by the observation that a feature detector such as

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:49.850
a vertical edge detector that's useful in one part of the image is probably useful in

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:50.850
another part of the image.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:55.690
And what that means is that if you've figured out, say a 3 by 3 filter for detecting

00:02:55.690 --> 00:03:02.810
vertical edges, you can then apply the same 3 by 3 filter over here and in the next

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:06.010
position over and in the next position over and so on.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:11.770
And so each of these feature detectors, each of these outputs can use the same

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:18.610
parameters in lots of different positions in your input image in order to detect, say, a

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:21.010
vertical edge or some other feature.

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:27.410
And I think this is true for low level features like edges as well as the high level

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:32.650
features like maybe detecting the eye that indicates a face or cat or something is there.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:38.370
But being able to share in this case, the same nine parameters to compute all 16 of

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:43.090
these outputs is one of the ways the number of parameters is reduced.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:49.130
And it also just seems intuitive that a feature detector like a vertical edge detector

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:51.890
computed for the upper left hand corner of the image.

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:56.570
The same feature seems like it will probably be useful, has a good chance of being

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:58.930
useful for the lower right hand corner of the image.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:03.170
So maybe you don't need to learn separate feature detectors for the upper left and

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:05.010
the lower right hand corners of the image.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:09.450
And maybe you do have a dataset where the upper left hand corner and the lower right

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:11.930
hand corner have different distributions.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:15.530
So they maybe look a little bit different, but they might be similar enough.

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:19.810
They're sharing feature detectors in all across the image works just fine.

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:25.970
The second way that confidence get away with having relatively few parameters is by

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:27.410
having sparse connections.

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:28.330
So here's what I mean.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:32.890
If you look at this zero, this is computed via 3x3 convolution.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:37.930
And so it depends only on this 3x3 input grid of cells.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:45.850
So it's as if this output unit on the right is connected only to 9 out of these

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:49.530
6x6, 36 input features.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:54.650
And in particular, the rest of these pixel values, you know, all of these

00:04:54.650 --> 00:05:00.050
pixel values, all of these pixel values do not have any input on this, do not

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:01.810
have any effect on that output.

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:04.570
So that's what I mean by sparsity of connections.

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:15.290
As another example, this output depends only on these nine input features.

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:19.890
And so it's as if only those nine input features are connected to this output

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:23.050
and the other pixels just don't affect this output at all.

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:29.650
And so through these two mechanisms, a neural network has a lot fewer parameters,

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:32.930
which allows it to be trained with smaller training sets and is less

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:34.850
prone to be overfitting.

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:39.130
And sometimes you also hear about convolutional neural networks being

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:41.810
very good at capturing translation invariance.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:47.170
And that's the observation that a picture of a cat shifted a couple of

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:50.050
pixels to the right is still pretty clearly a cat.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:56.930
And convolutional structure helps the neural network encode the fact that

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:02.570
an image shifted a few pixels should result in pretty similar features and

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:06.770
should probably be assigned the same output label.

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:11.570
And the fact that you're applying the same filter, you know, is all the

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:15.970
positions of the image, both in the earlier layers and in the later layers

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:20.970
that helps a neural network automatically learn to be more robust or to

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:26.810
capture, to better capture this desirable property of translation invariance.

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:33.980
So these are maybe a couple of reasons why convolutions or convolutional

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:36.700
neural networks work so well in computer vision.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:41.660
Finally, let's put it all together and see how you can train one of these

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:46.980
networks. Let's say you want to build a cat detector and you have a label

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:53.540
training set as follows where now X is a image and the Y's can be

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:56.500
binary labels or one of K classes.

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:02.090
And let's say you've chosen a convolutional neural network structure,

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:06.810
maybe start with the image and then having convolutional and pooling

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:10.610
layers and then some fully connected layers followed by a softmax

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:13.090
output that then outputs Y hat.

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:19.090
The conv layers and the fully connected layers will have various

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:25.410
parameters, W as well as biases B. And so any setting of the

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:30.090
parameters therefore lets you define a cost function similar to

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:34.850
what we had seen in the previous causes where with randomly

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:41.090
initialized parameters W and B, you can compute the cost J as the

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:45.610
sum of losses of neural networks predictions on your entire

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:48.530
training set, maybe divided by M.

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:54.690
So to train this neural network, all you need to do is then use

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:58.530
gradient descent or some of the algorithm like gradient

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:02.850
December momentum or RMS problem or add them or something

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:06.170
else in order to optimize all the parameters in the neural

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:10.250
network to try to reduce the cost function J. And you find that

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:15.010
if you do this, you can build a very effective cat detector or

00:08:15.010 --> 00:08:16.770
some other detector.

